From ddf2c42179a2c5a5cacc34e8e8a16e6279de50cf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Longwei Yang <19267252+Longwei-Yang@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 23:48:19 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 40/41] Safarium: native permission prompts under the address
 field
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Ordinary permission requests ("example.com wants to: know your location,
show notifications, use your camera…") now show as an NSPopover under the
current tab's address field instead of Chrome's bubble centered over the
page. Chrome's factory gets a small hook (SetNativePermissionPromptFactory)
used where it would create its anchored bubble, including the quiet-request
fallback; Safarium registers its prompt there.

The popover has Chrome's text and choices (Allow while visiting the site /
Allow this time / Never allow, or Allow / Block) and calls the request
delegate the same way. Like Chrome's bubble it stays until answered (Esc
dismisses), starts with no button focused, and ignores clicks in the first
half second after it appears or its window comes forward. Switching tabs
hides it and keeps the request pending. Requests it does not model (storage
access across two sites, smart cards, approximate location) and pages
without an address field on screen keep Chrome's bubble.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
